/*
 MongoDB Delete Operations — Notes
 
This file demonstrates how to remove documents and databases in MongoDB.

1. Deletion Methods:
   - deleteOne(): Removes the first document that matches a filter.
   - deleteMany(): Removes all documents matching a filter.
   - dropDatabase(): Deletes the entire database, including all collections and documents.

2. Important Notes:
   - Be cautious while using deleteMany() and dropDatabase() as they can remove large amounts of data permanently.
   - Always verify your filters before deleting documents.
*/

// Delete one document where city = "Mumbai"
// Removes the first matching document found in the collection.
db.student.deleteOne({city: "Mumbai"});

// Delete one document where marks < 75
// Removes the first document with marks less than 75.
db.student.deleteOne({marks: {$lt: 75}});

// Delete all documents where city = "Delhi"
// Removes every document that matches the filter condition.
db.student.deleteMany({city: "Delhi"});

// Delete all documents from the "student" collection
// Passing an empty filter {} removes all documents but keeps the collection itself.
db.student.deleteMany({}); // Empty the collection

// Drop the entire database
// Permanently deletes the currently selected database along with all its collections.
db.dropDatabase(); // Drops the current database

// Verify remaining data
// This should return an empty result if all documents were deleted successfully.
db.student.find();